## 🟡 Bugs That Don't Break Functionality (But Are Bad Code)

### 4. Global Variable Misuse in Augmentation

**File:** [01_data_augmentation/augmentation.ipynb](file:///home/tej/code/tobacco/Copy%20of%20Integration%20of%20Traditional%20Knowledge%20and%20Modern%20Science/Integration%20of%20Traditional%20Knowledge%20and%20Modern%20Science/01_data_augmentation/augmentation.ipynb) (Cell 3)

```python
image_list = ""  # ← Initialized as empty string at module level

def augment_images_from_random_file(class_dir, num_samples=5):
    image_files = [f for f in image_list if ...]  # ← Reads GLOBAL variable
    # Should be: image_files = [f for f in os.listdir(class_dir) if ...]
```

The function depends on a global variable that gets overwritten in the loop below. This is fragile — if the function is ever called out of order or independently, it will silently use stale data.

---

### 5. Augmentation Overshoot

**File:** [01_data_augmentation/augmentation.ipynb](file:///home/tej/code/tobacco/Copy%20of%20Integration%20of%20Traditional%20Knowledge%20and%20Modern%20Science/Integration%20of%20Traditional%20Knowledge%20and%20Modern%20Science/01_data_augmentation/augmentation.ipynb) (Cell 3)

```python
while images_needed > 0:
    augment_images_from_random_file(class_dir, num_samples=5)
    images_needed -= 5  # Always decrements by 5, even if only 2 were needed
```

If a class needs 502 images and has 500, it generates 5 extra (target was 2). Minor but leads to class counts of 505, 503, etc. instead of exactly 500.

---

### 6. Model File Naming Confusion

Model files are named `model_inceptionv2_*.h5` but the architecture is **MobileNetV2**, not InceptionV2/V3. Code comments also reference "InceptionV3 input shape" while using MobileNetV2.

---

### 7. Plaintext Password Storage (Security)

**File:** [03_web_app/database.py](file:///home/tej/code/tobacco/Copy%20of%20Integration%20of%20Traditional%20Knowledge%20and%20Modern%20Science/Integration%20of%20Traditional%20Knowledge%20and%20Modern%20Science/03_web_app/database.py)

Passwords are stored as plaintext in SQLite. Any database leak exposes all user passwords directly.

```python
# CURRENT: Direct storage
cursor.execute("INSERT INTO users ... VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?)", (username, email, password, ...))

# SHOULD BE: Hashed with bcrypt
import bcrypt
hashed = bcrypt.hashpw(password.encode(), bcrypt.gensalt())
```

---

### 8. Hardcoded Flask Secret Key

**File:** [03_web_app/config.py](file:///home/tej/code/tobacco/Copy%20of%20Integration%20of%20Traditional%20Knowledge%20and%20Modern%20Science/Integration%20of%20Traditional%20Knowledge%20and%20Modern%20Science/03_web_app/config.py)

```python
SECRET_KEY = "supersecretkey"  # Anyone who reads the source can forge sessions
```

Should use `os.environ.get("SECRET_KEY")` or `secrets.token_hex(32)`.

---

### 9. No CSRF Protection on Forms

POST endpoints (`/login`, `/add_user`, `/upload`) have no CSRF token validation. An attacker could craft a form that submits to your endpoints from any website.

